                <td width=572 valign=center colspan=10 style="width:343.6000pt;padding:0.0000pt 5.4000pt 0.0000pt 5.4000pt ;border-left:none;
mso-border-left-alt:none;border-right:1.0000pt solid windowtext;mso-border-right-alt:0.5000pt solid windowtext;
border-top:none;mso-border-top-alt:0.5000pt solid windowtext;border-bottom:1.0000pt solid windowtext;
mso-border-bottom-alt:0.5000pt solid windowtext;">
                    <p class=MsoNormal><span style="font-family:仿宋;mso-bidi-font-family:'Times New Roman';font-size:12.0000pt;
mso-font-kerning:1.0000pt;"><font face="仿宋"><p>This course integrates fundamental knowledge from histology, embryology, physiology, pathology, and pharmacology with the principles of integrated Chinese and Western medicine. Through a combination of didactic lectures, laboratory sessions, and case-based discussions, it systematically explores the normal structure and physiological functions of the endocrine and reproductive systems, while examining the pathological changes, disease progression mechanisms, and common therapeutic interventions for major related disorders. With an emphasis on high-prevalence diseases, the curriculum highlights the correlation between morphological alterations and functional impairments, underscores the interplay between localized pathologies and systemic effects, and prioritizes mechanistic understanding of disease pathogenesis as a core learning objective.</p></font></span><span style="font-family:'Times New Roman';mso-fareast-font-family:仿宋;font-size:10.5000pt;
mso-font-kerning:1.0000pt;"><o:p></o:p></span></p>
                </td>
            </tr>
